(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-allergy-inflammation-quiz"]={"title":"Allergy, Inflammation & Dry Eye Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Besides drug therapy, which physical interventions are offered for dry eye?","choices":["Warm compresses and lid massage only","Contact lens wear to protect the surface","Punctal plugs and surgical occlusion","Intravitreal injection of tear substitute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The same trick used to boost drug absorption is used here for tears themselves — keep what little there is on the eye rather than letting it drain. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the main uses of ocular glucocorticoids?","choices":["First-line treatment of all allergic conjunctivitis","Severe allergy, uveitis and post-surgical inflammation","Bacterial keratitis, to limit scarring","Chronic dry eye as maintenance therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They are generally reserved for refractory symptoms, and can be given topically or intraocularly depending on the indication. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"For which patient are mast cell stabilisers described as most useful?","choices":["Someone needing relief from an acute allergic flare","Someone with postoperative ocular inflammation","Predictable seasonal allergy, intolerant of alternatives","Someone with bacterial conjunctivitis and marked itching"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Their weaknesses — 5 to 14 days to full effect and often four times daily dosing — are tolerable if the season is predictable and can be pre-empted, and if the alternatives are not an option. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Ocular H1 agents are described as not really antagonists. What are they?","choices":["Non-competitive antagonists that bind irreversibly","Partial agonists producing a submaximal histamine response","Mast cell stabilisers that prevent histamine release","Inverse agonists, still competing with histamine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A true antagonist would simply occupy the site; an inverse agonist drives the receptor into an inactive state. They remain competitive with histamine, so the effect still depends on relative concentrations. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How long may an over-the-counter ocular vasoconstrictor be used, and what should the patient be told if there is no improvement?","choices":["Up to three months, with review if symptoms persist","Under two weeks; see a provider if no better in 72 hours","Indefinitely, as they are available without prescription","Less than two weeks, but no review is needed if symptoms persist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Short-term use only, because of rebound hyperaemia. And the 72-hour rule exists because a red eye that is not improving may be something more serious than allergy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"How does the technique for a gel or ointment differ from a drop?","choices":["The lacrimal duct must be covered for twice as long","The eye should be held open rather than closed","It should be applied directly to the cornea rather than the pocket","A ribbon into the pocket, with no duct occlusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The process is otherwise the same, but ointment does not drain away like a solution, so the punctal occlusion step is unnecessary. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which of these is an ocular antihistamine available over the counter?","choices":["Cromolyn","Bromfenac","Ketotifen","Loteprednol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the one marked over the counter in the antihistamine list, which matters because it is what a patient may already have tried before they reach you. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient has dry eye in the context of rheumatoid arthritis. What should be done first?","choices":["Start cyclosporine drops immediately","Treat the underlying disease","Insert punctal plugs","Prescribe a topical steroid pulse"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dry eyes are often a manifestation of something systemic — Sjogren syndrome, rheumatoid arthritis, vitamin A deficiency, Stevens-Johnson syndrome — and treating the eye without addressing the cause treats a symptom. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A patient has acute itchy, watery eyes from a sudden pollen exposure and wants relief today. Which class is appropriate, and why?","choices":["An antihistamine, because the onset is within minutes","A mast cell stabiliser, because it prevents histamine release","A topical glucocorticoid, because it suppresses the allergic response","An ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory, because it blocks prostaglandins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines work within minutes, which is what an acute presentation needs. They are also typically preferred over mast cell stabilisers, though two weeks of therapy is needed to judge full efficacy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 44 and 47"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs matters most in a patient who also has glaucoma?","choices":["Increased tear production","Rebound hyperaemia","Cataract formation","A rise in intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lacrimation, keratitis and irritation are the nuisance effects; the pressure rise is the one that interacts with an existing diagnosis and could undo glaucoma control. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What are the ocular indications for a topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory, and what is it NOT recommended for?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis, to reduce discomfort while antibiotics work","Chronic dry eye with inflammation","Fungal keratitis, to limit scarring","Postoperative inflammation and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Their place is after surgery and in allergic disease; they are explicitly not routinely recommended for conjunctivitis generally. Their adverse effects include lacrimation, keratitis and a rise in intraocular pressure. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has conjunctivitis. What is the advice about resuming lens wear?","choices":["Stop, and resume after 24 hours clear of inflammation","Continue wearing them, since the drops will treat the eye anyway","Stop, and resume as soon as the drops are finished","Stop permanently and switch to spectacles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three parts matter — stop, wait for a clear 24 hours, and deal with the lens itself. Eye makeup should go too, since it is the other reservoir. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient starts an ocular antihistamine. How long before they feel relief, and how long before its full effect can be judged?","choices":["Five to fourteen days before any effect at all","Minutes for relief, two weeks for full efficacy","Full effect immediately, with no further change","Six weeks, as with most topical ocular therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter in counselling — the drug does something immediately, so it will not feel useless, but the final verdict takes a fortnight. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Besides suppressing inflammation, what other effect of glucocorticoids is useful in the eye?","choices":["They inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation","They increase aqueous outflow, lowering pressure","They promote corneal epithelial healing","They have antimicrobial activity against surface organisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a structure where a scar in the wrong place costs vision, limiting collagen deposition is a benefit in its own right — and it is part of why they are used after surgery. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does the vasoconstrictor component of an over-the-counter allergy drop reduce redness?","choices":["It activates alpha-adrenergic receptors on vessels","It blocks histamine at the H1 receptor","It stabilises mast cells to prevent degranulation","It inhibits cyclooxygenase in the conjunctiva"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Constricting those vessels reduces conjunctival oedema and the visible redness. It treats the appearance rather than the allergy, which is why it is paired with an antihistamine in products such as Naphcon-A. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"By what mechanism do ocular glucocorticoids suppress the late-phase allergic reaction?","choices":["They block cyclooxygenase only","They inhibit interleukin-2 production","They inhibit phospholipase A2","They stabilise mast cell membranes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cutting off arachidonic acid stops the whole downstream cascade, not just the prostaglandin arm. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, which is why they reduce scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
